      "s": "TechCrunch",
      "t": "Rivian spinout Also was working on an e-moto project in Kenya &#8212; until it walked away",
      "d": "Also co-founder and president Chris Yu told TechCrunch that he wanted to prioritize autonomous vehicles.",
      "u": "https://techcrunch.com/2026/11/03/rivian-spinout-also-owns-up-to-mistakes-in-delayed-e-bike-launch/",
      "m": "11. 03. PM 04:49",
      "ts": 1793724549000,
      "fullText": "Also, the micromobility startup spun out of Rivian in 2025, quietly started and then walked away from an electric two-wheeler pilot project in Kenya earlier this year, as it sought to focus its resources on its consumer e-bike and autonomous vehicle ambitions, TechCrunch has learned. The project received grant funding from the Shell Foundation, an independent charity established by the British oil and gas giant Shell plc. A section of the Shell Foundation&#8217;s website that has since been removed said the project&#8217;s goal was to &#8220;enable and de-risk global OEM entry into Africa&#8217;s EV market by piloting a locally integrated electric two-wheeler platform in Kenya, with potential to scale across East Africa.&#8221; According to the deleted section, Also was expected to work on modular battery designs and distributed EV charging, and planned on &#8220;developing a purpose-built electric motorcycle for African riders.&#8221; The opportunity for electric motorcycles seems ripe on the continent, which has a massive two-wheeler market. African mobility company Spiro recently said it has more than 130,000 electric motorcycles operating across seven countries, along with 2,500 battery-swapping stations. This boom has drawn interest from Chinese mobility companies and investors, which are pouring resources into the space . In a September email, Also told TechCrunch that it had &#8220;explored this project&#8221; with an unnamed partner, but ultimately &#8220;decided to focus on its core roadmap&#8221; after completing only &#8220;some early-stage work.&#8221; Also said the grant funding it received from the Shell Foundation &#8220;will follow the project to support its ongoing development,&#8221; but declined to offer specifics. The Shell Foundation told TechCrunch in an email that the project was &#8220;intended to generate evidence on driver income uplift potential, operational performance and commercial viability to deepen the company’s understanding of the "
